Understood. We will skip the database indexing and stick strictly to the Java application layer.
To answer your question directly: No, the response format will absolutely not change. The frontend will still receive the exact same JSON array of CommonReq objects. The only difference is that previously, every object in that array had "reqStatus": "PENDING". Now, some objects in that array will have "reqStatus": "QUEUE". Your frontend UI will just need to know how to render the QUEUE status (e.g., showing an "In Progress" chip).
Here is how we integrate this into your existing /pending-requests API safely.
To ensure we don't accidentally break your other screens (like Branch or Segment master) that might only expect PENDING requests, we can use a simple if condition. We will tell the code: "If this is an LHO Transfer, fetch PENDING and QUEUE. If it is anything else, just fetch PENDING."
1. Update the Repository (CommonRequestRepository.java)
We still need the In method so the database can search for a list of statuses. Replace your old method with this one:
    // Replace the old findByCreatorIdNotAndReqStatusAndReqType with this:
    List<CommonReq> findByCreatorIdNotAndReqStatusInAndReqType(
            String creatorId, 
            List<RequestStatus> statuses, 
            RequestType reqType
    );

2. Update the Existing Service (RequestServiceImpl.java)
Modify your existing getPendingRequests method. You do not need a new controller or a new interface method.
    @Override
    public List<CommonReq> getPendingRequests(
            String userId,
            RequestType requestType
    ) {
        if (requestType == null) {
            log.error("Request type is missing. Cannot fetch pending requests for user: {}", userId);
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "Request type is missing, Please provide correct request type to fetch pending requests."
            );
        }
        log.info("Fetching pending requests for user: {} with type: {}", userId, requestType);

        List<RequestStatus> targetStatuses;

        // Smart Routing: Only LHO gets both P and Q. Everything else stays exactly as it was.
        if (requestType == RequestType.LHO_TRANSFER) {
            targetStatuses = List.of(RequestStatus.PENDING, RequestStatus.QUEUE);
        } else {
            targetStatuses = List.of(RequestStatus.PENDING);
        }

        // Call the updated repository method
        return commonRequestRepository.findByCreatorIdNotAndReqStatusInAndReqType(
                userId, 
                targetStatuses, 
                requestType
        );
    }

By doing this, your Controller stays exactly the same, your API endpoint (/pending-requests) stays exactly the same, the JSON response format stays exactly the same, and your other modules are completely protected.
